1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,826
Singapore 'Sold' Christmas Island

2
00:00:02,906 --> 00:00:05,900
in 1958 — Except It Never Owned It

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,165
Ask most Singaporeans about
Christmas Island and you'll get

4
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:14,362
some version of the same story:
Singapore used to own it,

5
00:00:14,442 --> 00:00:19,450
and sold it off to Australia in
1958 for far less than it was worth.

6
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:24,345
It's a tidy story — a small colonial
outpost cashing in an island,

7
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:29,250
a year before Singapore even got
a real vote in its own affairs.

8
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:31,250
It's also wrong.

9
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:33,965
Singapore never owned Christmas Island,

10
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:36,625
so there was nothing for it to sell.

11
00:00:36,625 --> 00:00:40,359
Sovereignty over Christmas
Island belonged to the

12
00:00:40,439 --> 00:00:44,875
British Crown from the moment
Britain annexed it in 1888.

13
00:00:44,875 --> 00:00:48,553
When it was folded into the
Straits Settlements in 1900,

14
00:00:48,633 --> 00:00:51,706
Singapore was named the
administering authority

15
00:00:51,786 --> 00:00:55,330
purely as a matter of
convenience — it was the nearest

16
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:59,100
seat of British government
in the region, nothing more.

17
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:03,275
Singapore's own leaders
said as much at the time.

18
00:01:03,275 --> 00:01:07,048
When the 1958 transfer to
Australia came up for debate,

19
00:01:07,128 --> 00:01:12,303
the colony's Acting Chief Minister,
Abdul Hamid Jumat, told the Legislative

20
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:17,277
Assembly plainly that Christmas Island
had never belonged to Singapore:

21
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,350
"The Colony was asked to
administer it and nothing more."

22
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:25,462
No Singaporean government,
colonial or postcolonial,

23
00:01:25,542 --> 00:01:27,800
ever had title to give away.

24
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:32,562
What Singapore did have was a share
of the island's phosphate income,

25
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:36,150
and that's what was actually
on the table in 1958.

26
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:41,296
Phosphate mining began in 1897,
and by 1900 the island was shipping

27
00:01:41,376 --> 00:01:46,210
ore to Japan and Germany, worked
largely by indentured Chinese,

28
00:01:46,290 --> 00:01:51,750
Malay, and Sikh labourers under
British and Australian mine operators.

29
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:56,378
The revenue — royalties and tax on
the mining company's operations —

30
00:01:56,458 --> 00:01:59,287
flowed to whoever administered the island,

31
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:04,075
which for over half a century meant
Singapore's colonial government.

32
00:02:04,075 --> 00:02:08,000
That arrangement survived
a wartime disaster.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,901
In March 1942, a mutiny among the
island's Indian garrison troops,

34
00:02:12,981 --> 00:02:15,543
swayed by Japanese propaganda about

35
00:02:15,623 --> 00:02:19,770
liberating India from British
rule, left several British

36
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:23,850
soldiers dead and the
remaining Europeans imprisoned.

37
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:27,654
When Japanese forces arrived
on the 31st of March,

38
00:02:27,734 --> 00:02:31,075
they took the island
without firing a shot.

39
00:02:31,075 --> 00:02:35,243
The episode exposed just how
vulnerable a British-administered

40
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:39,559
phosphate rock in the middle of
the Indian Ocean actually was —

41
00:02:39,639 --> 00:02:44,424
and it sharpened Australia's postwar
interest in controlling the supply

42
00:02:44,504 --> 00:02:49,300
directly rather than relying on
someone else's colony to guarantee it.

43
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,793
In 1948, the Australian and
New Zealand governments,

44
00:02:52,873 --> 00:02:57,328
acting through the British Phosphate
Commissioners, bought out the

45
00:02:57,408 --> 00:03:01,325
Christmas Island Phosphate
Company's operations outright.

46
00:03:01,325 --> 00:03:05,144
From there it was only a
matter of time before ownership

47
00:03:05,224 --> 00:03:09,669
followed the money: in June 1957,
Britain and Australia announced

48
00:03:09,749 --> 00:03:13,637
sovereignty would transfer to
Canberra, and the Christmas

49
00:03:13,717 --> 00:03:17,825
Island Bill cleared the UK
Parliament the following spring.

50
00:03:17,825 --> 00:03:23,884
On the 1st of October 1958, the island
formally became Australian territory —

51
00:03:23,964 --> 00:03:27,950
a date it still marks every
year as Territory Day.

52
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:31,784
Singapore's government, led by
Chief Minister Lim Yew Hock,

53
00:03:31,864 --> 00:03:35,765
received a lump-sum ex-gratia
payment reported at roughly 20

54
00:03:35,845 --> 00:03:40,011
million Malayan dollars (UK
parliamentary records cite a broadly

55
00:03:40,091 --> 00:03:43,991
similar sum in sterling), pitched
explicitly as compensation

56
00:03:44,071 --> 00:03:48,038
for lost phosphate royalty and
tax income — not as a purchase

57
00:03:48,118 --> 00:03:51,900
price for land it never held
title to in the first place.

58
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,977
At the time, Singapore was
collecting around 1.5 million

59
00:03:56,057 --> 00:04:00,580
Malayan dollars a year from the
island's phosphate operations,

60
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:04,000
with about 32 years left
on the mining lease.

61
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,733
Run that forward and the
income being signed away was

62
00:04:07,813 --> 00:04:11,907
worth in the neighbourhood of
48 million Malayan dollars —

63
00:04:11,987 --> 00:04:15,800
more than double the lump
sum Singapore actually got.

64
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:19,250
The timing makes the deal stranger still.

65
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:22,489
It was finalised entirely
by outgoing colonial

66
00:04:22,569 --> 00:04:25,302
administrators in London and Canberra —

67
00:04:25,382 --> 00:04:30,569
Singapore's own Legislative Assembly
could object, as Abdul Hamid Jumat's

68
00:04:30,649 --> 00:04:34,400
remarks show it did, but
it had no power to stop it.

69
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:39,886
Singapore wouldn't win full internal
self-government for another year,

70
00:04:39,966 --> 00:04:43,147
in 1959, with its own elected Legislative

71
00:04:43,227 --> 00:04:47,600
Assembly and Lee Kuan Yew's
PAP taking office that May.

72
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:51,046
The last chapter of Singapore's
administrative link to

73
00:04:51,126 --> 00:04:55,291
Christmas Island was written by
people who, within twelve months,

74
00:04:55,371 --> 00:04:58,375
would no longer be the
ones running the place.

75
00:04:58,375 --> 00:05:02,295
Christmas Island today has
nothing to do with phosphate,

76
00:05:02,375 --> 00:05:04,875
and even less to do with Singapore.

77
00:05:04,875 --> 00:05:09,246
Since 2008, its North West Point
facility has been Australia's

78
00:05:09,326 --> 00:05:13,339
flashpoint immigration detention
centre, built to process

79
00:05:13,419 --> 00:05:17,934
asylum seekers who arrived by
boat — one of the most politically

80
00:05:18,014 --> 00:05:22,250
fraught pieces of infrastructure
in Australian public life.

81
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:25,955
The island is also famous
for something far less grim:

82
00:05:26,035 --> 00:05:29,740
every wet season, tens of
millions of native red crabs

83
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:34,576
migrate from the rainforest to the
sea in a single synchronised wave,

84
00:05:34,656 --> 00:05:37,600
drawing naturalists from around the world.

85
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:40,296
Almost nobody making that connection —

86
00:05:40,376 --> 00:05:44,898
to detention policy or to crabs
— has any idea the rock they're

87
00:05:44,978 --> 00:05:48,850
picturing was once run out
of an office in Singapore.

88
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:52,771
Why it matters today:
Singapore didn't lose an island

89
00:05:52,851 --> 00:05:57,149
in 1958 — it lost the chance
to correct a story about one,

90
00:05:57,229 --> 00:06:00,400
and nobody in power since has bothered to.

91
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,318
The myth survives not
because it was ever true,

92
00:06:03,398 --> 00:06:06,635
but because it was more
interesting than the actual,

93
00:06:06,715 --> 00:06:10,398
more bureaucratic answer:
Singapore was never anything more

94
00:06:10,478 --> 00:06:14,050
than the landlord's agent,
and got paid out accordingly.
